================================================================================
python-modbus-poll -- Changelog 
================================================================================

v12 to v13

ARCHITECTURE
------------

Output handling
  v12 : Three globals: log_file_handle, ENABLE_TIMESTAMP, QUIET_MODE
  v13 : Logger class -- instantiated once, passed to all functions

Receive logic
  v12 : s.recv(260) -- single read, silent truncation possible on slow links
  v13 : recv_modbus_response() -- reads MBAP header first, then loops for
        exactly the right number of bytes (pdu_length - 1 after header)

Connection model
  v12 : New TCP socket per poll, no retry
  v13 : Retry loop with configurable attempts; persistent socket in continuous polling mode


BUG FIXES
---------

recv_modbus_response -- length-aware receive
  v12 : s.recv(260) assumes the full response arrives in one TCP read.
        On slow or fragmented links this silently truncates the response.
  v13 : Reads the 7-byte MBAP header first, extracts the Length field,
        then loops until exactly pdu_length - 1 additional bytes are
        received. The -1 subtracts the Unit ID byte already consumed
        by the header read.

FC01 / FC02 -- coil and discrete input parsing
  v12 : parse_modbus_response() unconditionally treats all response data
        as 16-bit register pairs. FC01/FC02 responses are bit-packed
        (8 coils per byte, LSB first) -- the v12 parser would
        misinterpret or error on these responses.
  v13 : parse_modbus_response() detects FC01/FC02 and unpacks bits
        correctly, trims padding bits to the requested count, and routes
        the result to the new decode_coils() display function.


NEW ARGUMENTS
-------------

  --interval N    Poll continuously every N seconds (default: 0 = single
                  shot). Ctrl-C stops cleanly with poll count reported.
                  TCP connection is kept alive between polls and
                  reconnected automatically on failure.

  --retries N     Retry attempts on any socket/IO failure (default: 3).
                  Each failed attempt waits 1 second before retrying.

  --timeout N     Per-attempt socket timeout in seconds (default: 5).
                  Useful for slow serial gateways which may need 10+.

--analyze renamed to --verbose
  The flag that enables full register breakdown was renamed from
  --analyze to --verbose for clarity. Behaviour is identical.

Modbus spec count guard
  --count is now validated against Modbus specification limits:
    FC01 / FC02 : maximum 2000 coils
    FC03 / FC04 : maximum 125 registers
  If the supplied value exceeds the limit it is clamped with a printed
  warning before the request is sent.


NEW DECODE CAPABILITIES (--verbose mode)
-----------------------------------------

BCD -- Binary Coded Decimal (per register)
  Each 16-bit register is decoded as packed BCD (4 nibbles = 4 decimal
  digits). If any nibble is > 9 the register is flagged as invalid BCD.
  Common in older PLCs: Siemens S5, Mitsubishi, Omron.
  Example: 0x1234 -> 1234

IPv4 (per 32-bit pair)
  Every pair of consecutive registers is decoded as a packed IPv4
  address (hi byte / lo byte per register, ABCD order).
  Example: r0=0xC0A8, r1=0x0101 -> 192.168.1.1

Unix epoch -> datetime (32-bit and 64-bit)
  All integer interpretations (32-bit and 64-bit, all endian formats)
  are checked against a plausible year 2000-2100 window. If plausible,
  the UTC datetime is shown alongside the raw integer value. 64-bit
  values are also checked as millisecond epochs (common in Java-
  originated industrial systems).

64-bit types -- FLOAT64, INT64, UINT64
  Whenever 4 or more registers are polled, groups of 4 consecutive
  registers are decoded as 64-bit types in four word-order formats,
  mirroring the 32-bit naming convention extended to 4 words:

    ABCDEFGH  Big Endian standard
    GHEFCDAB  Big Endian word-swap
    BADCFEHG  Little Endian byte-swap
    HGFEDCBA  Little Endian full reverse

UTF-16-BE and UTF-16-LE (multi-register summary)
  v12 : Only utf-16-be shown, labelled "Full UTF16"
  v13 : Both "Full UTF16-BE" and "Full UTF16-LE" shown explicitly.
        Byte order genuinely changes decoded text for UTF-16, unlike
        ASCII/UTF-8 where endian swapping is meaningless.

FC01 / FC02 -- coil display via decode_coils()
  New dedicated display function for coil and discrete input results:
    - Always shows a compact bit summary line and raw hex bytes
    - In --verbose mode additionally lists every coil address with
      its ON [1] / OFF [0] state


CODE STRUCTURE
--------------

  v12 decode_registers()     Single monolithic function
  v13                        Split into:
                               decode_register_16bit()
                               decode_32bit_block()
                               decode_64bit_block()
                               decode_multi_register_summary()

  v12 parse_modbus_response()  Registers only, no count parameter
  v13                          Handles FC01/FC02 bits and FC03/FC04
                               registers; takes optional count parameter

  v12  Single-shot only
  v13  Continuous polling loop with persistent socket and clean
       Ctrl-C handling

  v12  --analyze flag
  v13  Renamed to --verbose


WHAT DID NOT CHANGE
-------------------

  - All original epilog examples and notes preserved verbatim
  - Modbus exception code table and detection logic (0x80 bit check)
  - build_modbus_request() framing
  - reorder_bytes_for_format() 32-bit endian logic
  - --offsetminus1, --raw, --log, --timestamp, --quiet, --slave,
    --port, --floatformat -- behaviour unchanged
  - Raw frame display (show_raw_frames) -- same output, parameter
    renamed analyze -> verbose internally
  - --regfunction accepts 01/02/03/04 numeric FC codes in both versions


